#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:10
msgid "4.3 Beyond Single Layers: Stacking for Deep Learning"
msgstr "4.3 Beyond Single Layers: Stacking for Deep Learning"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:12
msgid ""
"The Restricted Boltzmann Machine, as presented in Section [4.2 Restricted "
"Boltzmann Machine (RBM)](kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-restricted.md), is a"
" powerful generative model with a single layer of latent variables. However,"
" many real-world datasets, i.e. images, speech, natural language, exhibit "
"**hierarchical structure**. Low-level features (edges, phonemes, word "
"fragments) combine to form mid-level features (shapes, syllables, phrases), "
"which in turn combine to form high-level concepts (objects, words, "
"semantics). A single-layer latent variable model, no matter how many hidden "
"units it possesses, may struggle to capture such multi-level abstractions "
"efficiently. This limitation motivates the **stacking** of RBMs to form "
"**Deep Belief Networks (DBNs)** and **Deep Boltzmann Machines (DBMs)**. "
"Stacking transforms a shallow generative model into a deep, hierarchical "
"one, capable of learning progressively more abstract representations of the "
"data. This section describes the greedy layer-wise training procedure that "
"made deep generative models practical, and explains how stacked RBMs connect"
" to the broader landscape of deep learning."
msgstr ""
"The Restricted Boltzmann Machine, as presented in Section [4.2 Restricted "
"Boltzmann Machine (RBM)](kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-restricted.md), is a"
" powerful generative model with a single layer of latent variables. However,"
" many real-world datasets, i.e. images, speech, natural language, exhibit "
"**hierarchical structure**. Low-level features (edges, phonemes, word "
"fragments) combine to form mid-level features (shapes, syllables, phrases), "
"which in turn combine to form high-level concepts (objects, words, "
"semantics). A single-layer latent variable model, no matter how many hidden "
"units it possesses, may struggle to capture such multi-level abstractions "
"efficiently. This limitation motivates the **stacking** of RBMs to form "
"**Deep Belief Networks (DBNs)** and **Deep Boltzmann Machines (DBMs)**. "
"Stacking transforms a shallow generative model into a deep, hierarchical "
"one, capable of learning progressively more abstract representations of the "
"data. This section describes the greedy layer-wise training procedure that "
"made deep generative models practical, and explains how stacked RBMs connect"
" to the broader landscape of deep learning."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:15
msgid "The Motivation: Hierarchical Feature Learning"
msgstr "The Motivation: Hierarchical Feature Learning"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:16
msgid ""
"Consider the task of modeling a collection of face images. A shallow RBM "
"with a single hidden layer might learn localized Gabor-like edge detectors "
"as its hidden features. These are useful, but they fail to capture the "
"higher-order regularities of faces: the spatial arrangement of eyes, nose, "
"and mouth, or the characteristic symmetries of facial structure."
msgstr ""
"Consider the task of modeling a collection of face images. A shallow RBM "
"with a single hidden layer might learn localized Gabor-like edge detectors "
"as its hidden features. These are useful, but they fail to capture the "
"higher-order regularities of faces: the spatial arrangement of eyes, nose, "
"and mouth, or the characteristic symmetries of facial structure."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:18
msgid ""
"A deep architecture, by contrast, can learn a **hierarchy of "
"representations**:"
msgstr ""
"A deep architecture, by contrast, can learn a **hierarchy of "
"representations**:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:20
msgid ""
"**First hidden layer**: Detects low-level features such as oriented edges "
"and corners."
msgstr ""
"**First hidden layer**: Detects low-level features such as oriented edges "
"and corners."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:21
msgid ""
"**Second hidden layer**: Combines edges to form mid-level features like "
"facial parts (eye, nose, mouth detectors)."
msgstr ""
"**Second hidden layer**: Combines edges to form mid-level features like "
"facial parts (eye, nose, mouth detectors)."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:22
msgid ""
"**Third hidden layer**: Combines facial parts to form high-level, abstract "
"representations of whole faces."
msgstr ""
"**Third hidden layer**: Combines facial parts to form high-level, abstract "
"representations of whole faces."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:24
msgid ""
"This hierarchical composition mirrors the structure of the visual cortex and"
" enables the model to represent complex data distributions with "
"exponentially fewer parameters than a shallow model of comparable "
"representational power."
msgstr ""
"This hierarchical composition mirrors the structure of the visual cortex and"
" enables the model to represent complex data distributions with "
"exponentially fewer parameters than a shallow model of comparable "
"representational power."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:26
msgid "Deep Belief Networks: Composition of RBMs"
msgstr "Deep Belief Networks: Composition of RBMs"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:27
msgid ""
"A **Deep Belief Network (DBN)** is a generative model composed of multiple "
"layers of stochastic, latent variables. The top two layers form an "
"**undirected** RBM, while the lower layers form a **directed** sigmoid "
"belief network. Specifically, a DBN with $L$ hidden layers is defined as:"
msgstr ""
"A **Deep Belief Network (DBN)** is a generative model composed of multiple "
"layers of stochastic, latent variables. The top two layers form an "
"**undirected** RBM, while the lower layers form a **directed** sigmoid "
"belief network. Specifically, a DBN with $L$ hidden layers is defined as:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:29
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The top two layers, $\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}$ and $\\mathbf{h}^{(L)}$, form an "
"RBM."
msgstr ""
"The top two layers, $\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}$ and $\\mathbf{h}^{(L)}$, form an "
"RBM."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:30
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The remaining layers are connected via directed, top-down generative "
"weights: $\\mathbf{h}^{(l)}$ generates $\\mathbf{h}^{(l-1)}$ for $l = L-1, "
"\\ldots, 1$, with $\\mathbf{h}^{(0)} = \\mathbf{v}$."
msgstr ""
"The remaining layers are connected via directed, top-down generative "
"weights: $\\mathbf{h}^{(l)}$ generates $\\mathbf{h}^{(l-1)}$ for $l = L-1, "
"\\ldots, 1$, with $\\mathbf{h}^{(0)} = \\mathbf{v}$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:32
msgid "The joint distribution factorizes as:"
msgstr "The joint distribution factorizes as:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:34
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"P(\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{h}^{(1)}, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}^{(L)}) = "
"P(\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}, \\mathbf{h}^{(L)}) \\prod_{l=1}^{L-2} "
"P(\\mathbf{h}^{(l)} \\mid \\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)})"
msgstr ""
"P(\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{h}^{(1)}, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}^{(L)}) = "
"P(\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}, \\mathbf{h}^{(L)}) \\prod_{l=1}^{L-2} "
"P(\\mathbf{h}^{(l)} \\mid \\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)})"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:36
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"where $P(\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}, \\mathbf{h}^{(L)})$ is the RBM distribution at"
" the top, and the conditional distributions $P(\\mathbf{h}^{(l)} \\mid "
"\\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)})$ are those of a sigmoid belief network."
msgstr ""
"where $P(\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}, \\mathbf{h}^{(L)})$ is the RBM distribution at"
" the top, and the conditional distributions $P(\\mathbf{h}^{(l)} \\mid "
"\\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)})$ are those of a sigmoid belief network."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:38
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The directed connections enable efficient **ancestral sampling**: to "
"generate a sample, one first samples from the top RBM to obtain "
"$\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}$ and $\\mathbf{h}^{(L)}$, then propagates downward "
"through the directed layers, sampling each layer in turn."
msgstr ""
"The directed connections enable efficient **ancestral sampling**: to "
"generate a sample, one first samples from the top RBM to obtain "
"$\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}$ and $\\mathbf{h}^{(L)}$, then propagates downward "
"through the directed layers, sampling each layer in turn."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:40
msgid "Greedy Layer-Wise Training"
msgstr "Greedy Layer-Wise Training"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:41
msgid ""
"Training a deep generative model with many layers of latent variables is "
"challenging due to the **explaining-away** effect and the intractability of "
"exact inference. The breakthrough that made DBNs practical was **greedy "
"layer-wise pre-training**, introduced by Hinton, Osindero, and Teh in 2006."
msgstr ""
"Training a deep generative model with many layers of latent variables is "
"challenging due to the **explaining-away** effect and the intractability of "
"exact inference. The breakthrough that made DBNs practical was **greedy "
"layer-wise pre-training**, introduced by Hinton, Osindero, and Teh in 2006."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:43
msgid ""
"The core idea is elegantly simple: **train one RBM at a time, and stack "
"them**."
msgstr ""
"The core idea is elegantly simple: **train one RBM at a time, and stack "
"them**."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:45
msgid "**Algorithm: Greedy Layer-Wise Training of a DBN**"
msgstr "**Algorithm: Greedy Layer-Wise Training of a DBN**"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:47
msgid "**Train the first RBM**:"
msgstr "**Train the first RBM**:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:48
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Treat the visible data $\\mathbf{v}$ as the visible layer and introduce a "
"hidden layer $\\mathbf{h}^{(1)}$."
msgstr ""
"Treat the visible data $\\mathbf{v}$ as the visible layer and introduce a "
"hidden layer $\\mathbf{h}^{(1)}$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:49
msgid "Train an RBM on the data using contrastive divergence."
msgstr "Train an RBM on the data using contrastive divergence."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:50
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"After training, the RBM defines a generative model $P(\\mathbf{v}, "
"\\mathbf{h}^{(1)})$ and an inference model $P(\\mathbf{h}^{(1)} \\mid "
"\\mathbf{v})$."
msgstr ""
"After training, the RBM defines a generative model $P(\\mathbf{v}, "
"\\mathbf{h}^{(1)})$ and an inference model $P(\\mathbf{h}^{(1)} \\mid "
"\\mathbf{v})$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:52
msgid "**Generate training data for the next layer**:"
msgstr "**Generate training data for the next layer**:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:53
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Use the trained inference model to compute the posterior probabilities "
"$P(\\mathbf{h}^{(1)} \\mid \\mathbf{v})$ for each training example."
msgstr ""
"Use the trained inference model to compute the posterior probabilities "
"$P(\\mathbf{h}^{(1)} \\mid \\mathbf{v})$ for each training example."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:54
msgid ""
"These posterior probabilities (or samples from them) become the \"data\" for"
" training the next RBM. They represent the data in the feature space learned"
" by the first hidden layer."
msgstr ""
"These posterior probabilities (or samples from them) become the \"data\" for"
" training the next RBM. They represent the data in the feature space learned"
" by the first hidden layer."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:56
msgid "**Train the second RBM**:"
msgstr "**Train the second RBM**:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:57
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Treat the vectors $\\mathbf{h}^{(1)}$ as visible units and introduce a "
"second hidden layer $\\mathbf{h}^{(2)}$."
msgstr ""
"Treat the vectors $\\mathbf{h}^{(1)}$ as visible units and introduce a "
"second hidden layer $\\mathbf{h}^{(2)}$."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:58
msgid "Train an RBM on these feature vectors."
msgstr "Train an RBM on these feature vectors."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:60
msgid "**Repeat** for as many layers as desired."
msgstr "**Repeat** for as many layers as desired."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:62
msgid ""
"After this unsupervised pre-training, the stacked RBMs can be converted into"
" a DBN by discarding the bottom-up recognition weights of all but the first "
"layer, and using the top-down generative weights to define the directed "
"model. Alternatively, weights can be used to initialize a deep neural "
"network for **discriminative fine-tuning** using backpropagation."
msgstr ""
"After this unsupervised pre-training, the stacked RBMs can be converted into"
" a DBN by discarding the bottom-up recognition weights of all but the first "
"layer, and using the top-down generative weights to define the directed "
"model. Alternatively, weights can be used to initialize a deep neural "
"network for **discriminative fine-tuning** using backpropagation."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:64
msgid "Why Greedy Layer-Wise Training Works"
msgstr "Why Greedy Layer-Wise Training Works"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:65
msgid ""
"The effectiveness of greedy layer-wise training can be understood from "
"several perspectives:"
msgstr ""
"The effectiveness of greedy layer-wise training can be understood from "
"several perspectives:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:67
msgid ""
"**Variational Lower Bound**: Adding a new hidden layer and training it as an"
" RBM on the inferred features of the previous layer can be shown to improve "
"a variational lower bound on the log-likelihood of the data under the "
"expanded model. Each additional layer tightens the bond."
msgstr ""
"**Variational Lower Bound**: Adding a new hidden layer and training it as an"
" RBM on the inferred features of the previous layer can be shown to improve "
"a variational lower bound on the log-likelihood of the data under the "
"expanded model. Each additional layer tightens the bond."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:68
msgid ""
"**Feature Abstraction**: Each RBM learns to model the distribution of "
"features produced by the layer below. Since these features are already "
"somewhat abstract and decorrelated, the next RBM can focus on capturing "
"higher-order dependencies among them."
msgstr ""
"**Feature Abstraction**: Each RBM learns to model the distribution of "
"features produced by the layer below. Since these features are already "
"somewhat abstract and decorrelated, the next RBM can focus on capturing "
"higher-order dependencies among them."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:69
msgid ""
"**Avoiding Local Minima**: Deep supervised networks trained from random "
"initializations often get stuck in poor local minima due to the complex, "
"non-convex loss landscape. Pre-training initializes the weights in a region "
"of parameter space that corresponds to a sensible generative model of the "
"data, providing a far better starting point for subsequent discriminative "
"fine-tuning."
msgstr ""
"**Avoiding Local Minima**: Deep supervised networks trained from random "
"initializations often get stuck in poor local minima due to the complex, "
"non-convex loss landscape. Pre-training initializes the weights in a region "
"of parameter space that corresponds to a sensible generative model of the "
"data, providing a far better starting point for subsequent discriminative "
"fine-tuning."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:71
msgid "Deep Boltzmann Machines (DBMs)"
msgstr "Deep Boltzmann Machines (DBMs)"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:72
msgid ""
"A closely related architecture is the **Deep Boltzmann Machine (DBM)**, "
"introduced by Salakhutdinov and Hinton in 2009. Unlike a DBN, where only the"
" top two layers form an undirected RBM, a DBM is a **fully undirected** "
"model with multiple hidden layers. All connections are symmetric, and there "
"are no directed edges."
msgstr ""
"A closely related architecture is the **Deep Boltzmann Machine (DBM)**, "
"introduced by Salakhutdinov and Hinton in 2009. Unlike a DBN, where only the"
" top two layers form an undirected RBM, a DBM is a **fully undirected** "
"model with multiple hidden layers. All connections are symmetric, and there "
"are no directed edges."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:74
msgid "The energy function for a DBM with two hidden layers is:"
msgstr "The energy function for a DBM with two hidden layers is:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:76
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"E(\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{h}^{(1)}, \\mathbf{h}^{(2)}) = -\\mathbf{v}^\\top "
"\\mathbf{W}^{(1)} \\mathbf{h}^{(1)} - (\\mathbf{h}^{(1)})^\\top "
"\\mathbf{W}^{(2)} \\mathbf{h}^{(2)} - \\mathbf{b}^\\top \\mathbf{v} - "
"(\\mathbf{c}^{(1)})^\\top \\mathbf{h}^{(1)} - (\\mathbf{c}^{(2)})^\\top "
"\\mathbf{h}^{(2)}"
msgstr ""
"E(\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{h}^{(1)}, \\mathbf{h}^{(2)}) = -\\mathbf{v}^\\top "
"\\mathbf{W}^{(1)} \\mathbf{h}^{(1)} - (\\mathbf{h}^{(1)})^\\top "
"\\mathbf{W}^{(2)} \\mathbf{h}^{(2)} - \\mathbf{b}^\\top \\mathbf{v} - "
"(\\mathbf{c}^{(1)})^\\top \\mathbf{h}^{(1)} - (\\mathbf{c}^{(2)})^\\top "
"\\mathbf{h}^{(2)}"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:78
msgid ""
"DBMs retain the full undirected nature of the Boltzmann machine, which makes"
" them more principled probabilistic models than DBNs. However, exact "
"inference is even more challenging, and training typically requires a more "
"sophisticated procedure, often involving mean-field approximations or "
"persistent contrastive divergence with carefully designed initialization."
msgstr ""
"DBMs retain the full undirected nature of the Boltzmann machine, which makes"
" them more principled probabilistic models than DBNs. However, exact "
"inference is even more challenging, and training typically requires a more "
"sophisticated procedure, often involving mean-field approximations or "
"persistent contrastive divergence with carefully designed initialization."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:80
msgid ""
"In practice, DBNs have been more widely adopted due to their simpler "
"training procedure and direct compatibility with feedforward neural network "
"initialization."
msgstr ""
"In practice, DBNs have been more widely adopted due to their simpler "
"training procedure and direct compatibility with feedforward neural network "
"initialization."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:82
msgid "From Unsupervised Pre-Training to Discriminative Fine-Tuning"
msgstr "From Unsupervised Pre-Training to Discriminative Fine-Tuning"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:83
msgid ""
"One of the most impactful applications of stacked RBMs was **unsupervised "
"pre-training for deep supervised learning**. Before the widespread adoption "
"of techniques like batch normalization, ReLU activations, and large labeled "
"datasets (e.g., ImageNet), training deep neural networks with many hidden "
"layers was notoriously difficult. Gradients would vanish or explode, and "
"optimization would stall in poor local minima."
msgstr ""
"One of the most impactful applications of stacked RBMs was **unsupervised "
"pre-training for deep supervised learning**. Before the widespread adoption "
"of techniques like batch normalization, ReLU activations, and large labeled "
"datasets (e.g., ImageNet), training deep neural networks with many hidden "
"layers was notoriously difficult. Gradients would vanish or explode, and "
"optimization would stall in poor local minima."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:85
msgid "The RBM pre-training pipeline provided a solution:"
msgstr "The RBM pre-training pipeline provided a solution:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:87
msgid ""
"**Pre-train** a stack of RBMs in an unsupervised manner on unlabeled data "
"(which was often abundant)."
msgstr ""
"**Pre-train** a stack of RBMs in an unsupervised manner on unlabeled data "
"(which was often abundant)."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:88
msgid ""
"**Unroll** the stacked RBMs to form a deep feedforward network with the same"
" weights. This network effectively implements the bottom-up inference "
"pathway."
msgstr ""
"**Unroll** the stacked RBMs to form a deep feedforward network with the same"
" weights. This network effectively implements the bottom-up inference "
"pathway."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:89
msgid "**Add a classification layer** on top of the highest-level features."
msgstr "**Add a classification layer** on top of the highest-level features."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:90
msgid ""
"**Fine-tune** the entire network using backpropagation on the labeled data."
msgstr ""
"**Fine-tune** the entire network using backpropagation on the labeled data."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:92
msgid ""
"This approach achieved state-of-the-art results on MNIST and, more "
"impressively, enabled the first significant reduction in error rates on the "
"TIMIT speech recognition benchmark. It demonstrated that deep architectures "
"could be trained effectively if initialized properly, paving the way for the"
" deep learning revolution."
msgstr ""
"This approach achieved state-of-the-art results on MNIST and, more "
"impressively, enabled the first significant reduction in error rates on the "
"TIMIT speech recognition benchmark. It demonstrated that deep architectures "
"could be trained effectively if initialized properly, paving the way for the"
" deep learning revolution."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:94
msgid "The End of the Pre-Training Era and the Legacy of Stacked RBMs"
msgstr "The End of the Pre-Training Era and the Legacy of Stacked RBMs"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:95
msgid ""
"By the early 2010s, advances in optimization (momentum, RMSprop, Adam), "
"activation functions (ReLU and variants), regularization (dropout), and the "
"availability of massive labeled datasets made unsupervised pre-training less"
" necessary for many supervised tasks. End-to-end supervised training of deep"
" networks from random initialization became the norm."
msgstr ""
"By the early 2010s, advances in optimization (momentum, RMSprop, Adam), "
"activation functions (ReLU and variants), regularization (dropout), and the "
"availability of massive labeled datasets made unsupervised pre-training less"
" necessary for many supervised tasks. End-to-end supervised training of deep"
" networks from random initialization became the norm."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:97
msgid "However, the conceptual legacy of stacked RBMs endures:"
msgstr "However, the conceptual legacy of stacked RBMs endures:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:99
msgid ""
"They established the **viability of deep architectures** and the importance "
"of **hierarchical feature learning**."
msgstr ""
"They established the **viability of deep architectures** and the importance "
"of **hierarchical feature learning**."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:100
msgid ""
"They demonstrated the value of **unsupervised representation learning** as a"
" precursor to supervised tasks."
msgstr ""
"They demonstrated the value of **unsupervised representation learning** as a"
" precursor to supervised tasks."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:101
msgid ""
"They introduced **contrastive divergence** and **greedy layer-wise "
"training**, which remain valuable tools in the generative modeling toolkit."
msgstr ""
"They introduced **contrastive divergence** and **greedy layer-wise "
"training**, which remain valuable tools in the generative modeling toolkit."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:102
msgid ""
"They inspired subsequent generative models, including **Variational "
"Autoencoders (VAEs)** and **Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)**, which "
"also learn hierarchical latent representations, albeit with different "
"training objectives."
msgstr ""
"They inspired subsequent generative models, including **Variational "
"Autoencoders (VAEs)** and **Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)**, which "
"also learn hierarchical latent representations, albeit with different "
"training objectives."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:104
msgid ""
"Moreover, in domains where labeled data is scarce but unlabeled data is "
"plentiful, unsupervised pre-training with RBMs or their modern descendants "
"(e.g., deep belief networks, deep Boltzmann machines) remains a relevant and"
" effective strategy."
msgstr ""
"Moreover, in domains where labeled data is scarce but unlabeled data is "
"plentiful, unsupervised pre-training with RBMs or their modern descendants "
"(e.g., deep belief networks, deep Boltzmann machines) remains a relevant and"
" effective strategy."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:106
msgid "Stacking Beyond RBMs: The General Principle"
msgstr "Stacking Beyond RBMs: The General Principle"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:107
msgid ""
"The principle of stacking simple modules to build deep, hierarchical models "
"extends far beyond RBMs. Modern deep learning is built on this exact idea:"
msgstr ""
"The principle of stacking simple modules to build deep, hierarchical models "
"extends far beyond RBMs. Modern deep learning is built on this exact idea:"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:109
msgid ""
"**Convolutional neural networks** stack convolutional layers to learn "
"increasingly complex visual features."
msgstr ""
"**Convolutional neural networks** stack convolutional layers to learn "
"increasingly complex visual features."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:110
msgid ""
"**Transformers** stack self-attention layers to capture hierarchical "
"linguistic structure."
msgstr ""
"**Transformers** stack self-attention layers to capture hierarchical "
"linguistic structure."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:111
msgid ""
"**Diffusion models** stack denoising steps to gradually refine generated "
"samples."
msgstr ""
"**Diffusion models** stack denoising steps to gradually refine generated "
"samples."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:113
msgid ""
"The RBM stack was the first practical demonstration that **greedy layer-wise"
" construction** could bootstrap the training of deep generative models. This"
" insight remains a cornerstone of deep learning methodology."
msgstr ""
"The RBM stack was the first practical demonstration that **greedy layer-wise"
" construction** could bootstrap the training of deep generative models. This"
" insight remains a cornerstone of deep learning methodology."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:115
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "Summary"

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:116
msgid ""
"**Stacking RBMs** forms **Deep Belief Networks (DBNs)** and **Deep Boltzmann"
" Machines (DBMs)**, enabling hierarchical feature learning."
msgstr ""
"**Stacking RBMs** forms **Deep Belief Networks (DBNs)** and **Deep Boltzmann"
" Machines (DBMs)**, enabling hierarchical feature learning."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:117
msgid ""
"**Greedy layer-wise training** trains one RBM at a time, using the inferred "
"features of one layer as data for the next."
msgstr ""
"**Greedy layer-wise training** trains one RBM at a time, using the inferred "
"features of one layer as data for the next."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:118
msgid ""
"DBNs combine an undirected top RBM with directed lower layers, while DBMs "
"are fully undirected."
msgstr ""
"DBNs combine an undirected top RBM with directed lower layers, while DBMs "
"are fully undirected."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:119
msgid ""
"Pre-trained stacks of RBMs provided effective **weight initializations** for"
" deep supervised networks, fueling early deep learning successes."
msgstr ""
"Pre-trained stacks of RBMs provided effective **weight initializations** for"
" deep supervised networks, fueling early deep learning successes."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:120
msgid ""
"Although largely supplanted by end-to-end supervised training on large "
"datasets, stacked RBMs remain historically significant and practically "
"useful in label-scarce regimes."
msgstr ""
"Although largely supplanted by end-to-end supervised training on large "
"datasets, stacked RBMs remain historically significant and practically "
"useful in label-scarce regimes."

#: ../../source/theoretical-foundations/kpp-theoretical-foundations-bm-deep.md:121
msgid ""
"The concept of **hierarchical representation learning** pioneered by stacked"
" RBMs is foundational to modern deep learning architectures."
msgstr ""
"The concept of **hierarchical representation learning** pioneered by stacked"
" RBMs is foundational to modern deep learning architectures."

#~ msgid ""
#~ "The Restricted Boltzmann Machine, as presented in Section [4.2 Restricted "
#~ "Boltzmann Machine (RBM)](/kpp-theoretical-foundationskpp-theoretical-"
#~ "foundations-bm-restricted), is a powerful generative model with a single "
#~ "layer of latent variables. However, many real-world datasets, i.e. images, "
#~ "speech, natural language, exhibit <b>hierarchical structure</b>. Low-level "
#~ "features (edges, phonemes, word fragments) combine to form mid-level "
#~ "features (shapes, syllables, phrases), which in turn combine to form high-"
#~ "level concepts (objects, words, semantics). A single-layer latent variable "
#~ "model, no matter how many hidden units it possesses, may struggle to capture"
#~ " such multi-level abstractions efficiently. This limitation motivates the "
#~ "<b>stacking</b> of RBMs to form <b>Deep Belief Networks (DBNs)</b> and "
#~ "<b>Deep Boltzmann Machines (DBMs)</b> . Stacking transforms a shallow "
#~ "generative model into a deep, hierarchical one, capable of learning "
#~ "progressively more abstract representations of the data. This section "
#~ "describes the greedy layer-wise training procedure that made deep generative"
#~ " models practical, and explains how stacked RBMs connect to the broader "
#~ "landscape of deep learning."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>The Motivation: Hierarchical Feature Learning</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "A deep architecture, by contrast, can learn a <b>hierarchy of "
#~ "representations</b>:"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>First hidden layer</b>: Detects low-level features such as oriented edges"
#~ " and corners."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Second hidden layer</b>: Combines edges to form mid-level features like "
#~ "facial parts (eye, nose, mouth detectors)."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Third hidden layer</b>: Combines facial parts to form high-level, "
#~ "abstract representations of whole faces."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Deep Belief Networks: Composition of RBMs</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "A <b>Deep Belief Network (DBN)</b> is a generative model composed of "
#~ "multiple layers of stochastic, latent variables. The top two layers form an "
#~ "<b>undirected</b> RBM, while the lower layers form a <b>directed</b> sigmoid"
#~ " belief network. Specifically, a DBN with $L$ hidden layers is defined as:"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "The directed connections enable efficient <b>ancestral sampling</b>: to "
#~ "generate a sample, one first samples from the top RBM to obtain "
#~ "$\\mathbf{h}^{(L-1)}$ and $\\mathbf{h}^{(L)}$, then propagates downward "
#~ "through the directed layers, sampling each layer in turn."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Greedy Layer-Wise Training</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "Training a deep generative model with many layers of latent variables is "
#~ "challenging due to the <b>explaining-away</b> effect and the intractability "
#~ "of exact inference. The breakthrough that made DBNs practical was <b>greedy "
#~ "layer-wise pre-training</b>, introduced by Hinton, Osindero, and Teh in "
#~ "2006."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "The core idea is elegantly simple: <b>train one RBM at a time, and stack "
#~ "them</b>."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Algorithm: Greedy Layer-Wise Training of a DBN</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Train the first RBM</b>:"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Generate training data for the next layer</b>:"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Train the second RBM</b>:"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Repeat</b> for as many layers as desired."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "After this unsupervised pre-training, the stacked RBMs can be converted into"
#~ " a DBN by discarding the bottom-up recognition weights of all but the first "
#~ "layer, and using the top-down generative weights to define the directed "
#~ "model. Alternatively, weights can be used to initialize a deep neural "
#~ "network for <b>discriminative fine-tuning</b> using backpropagation."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Why Greedy Layer-Wise Training Works</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Variational Lower Bound</b>: Adding a new hidden layer and training it as"
#~ " an RBM on the inferred features of the previous layer can be shown to "
#~ "improve a variational lower bound on the log-likelihood of the data under "
#~ "the expanded model. Each additional layer tightens the bond."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Feature Abstraction</b>: Each RBM learns to model the distribution of "
#~ "features produced by the layer below. Since these features are already "
#~ "somewhat abstract and decorrelated, the next RBM can focus on capturing "
#~ "higher-order dependencies among them."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Avoiding Local Minima</b>: Deep supervised networks trained from random "
#~ "initializations often get stuck in poor local minima due to the complex, "
#~ "non-convex loss landscape. Pre-training initializes the weights in a region "
#~ "of parameter space that corresponds to a sensible generative model of the "
#~ "data, providing a far better starting point for subsequent discriminative "
#~ "fine-tuning."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Deep Boltzmann Machines (DBMs)</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "A closely related architecture is the <b>Deep Boltzmann Machine (DBM)</b> , "
#~ "introduced by Salakhutdinov and Hinton in 2009. Unlike a DBN, where only the"
#~ " top two layers form an undirected RBM, a DBM is a <b>fully undirected "
#~ "</b>model with multiple hidden layers. All connections are symmetric, and "
#~ "there are no directed edges."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>From Unsupervised Pre-Training to Discriminative Fine-Tuning</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "One of the most impactful applications of stacked RBMs was <b>unsupervised "
#~ "pre-training for deep supervised learning</b>. Before the widespread "
#~ "adoption of techniques like batch normalization, ReLU activations, and large"
#~ " labeled datasets (e.g., ImageNet), training deep neural networks with many "
#~ "hidden layers was notoriously difficult. Gradients would vanish or explode, "
#~ "and optimization would stall in poor local minima."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Pre-train</b> a stack of RBMs in an unsupervised manner on unlabeled data"
#~ " (which was often abundant)."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Unroll</b> the stacked RBMs to form a deep feedforward network with the "
#~ "same weights. This network effectively implements the bottom-up inference "
#~ "pathway."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Add a classification layer</b> on top of the highest-level features."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Fine-tune</b> the entire network using backpropagation on the labeled "
#~ "data."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>The End of the Pre-Training Era and the Legacy of Stacked RBMs</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "They established the <b>viability of deep architectures</b> and the "
#~ "importance of <b>hierarchical feature learning</b>."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "They demonstrated the value of <b>unsupervised representation learning</b> "
#~ "as a precursor to supervised tasks."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "They introduced <b>contrastive divergence</b> and <b>greedy layer-wise "
#~ "training</b>, which remain valuable tools in the generative modeling "
#~ "toolkit."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "They inspired subsequent generative models, including <b>Variational "
#~ "Autoencoders (VAEs)</b> and <b>Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)</b>, "
#~ "which also learn hierarchical latent representations, albeit with different "
#~ "training objectives."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "<b>Stacking Beyond RBMs: The General Principle</b>"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Convolutional neural networks</b> stack convolutional layers to learn "
#~ "increasingly complex visual features."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Transformers</b> stack self-attention layers to capture hierarchical "
#~ "linguistic structure."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "<b>Diffusion models</b> stack denoising steps to gradually refine generated "
#~ "samples."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "The RBM stack was the first practical demonstration that <b>greedy layer-"
#~ "wise construction</b> could bootstrap the training of deep generative "
#~ "models. This insight remains a cornerstone of deep learning methodology."
#~ msgstr ""

